    "body": "How to use it in our team (read first): you work inside the team app, not in a code editor. You cannot run scripts, install packages, open a browser or a terminal – skip every step that needs them and apply the rules yourself. Deliver pages with make_file (one self-contained HTML file, Hebrew, RTL, mobile first, dark mode), new images with make_image (each one waits for the owner's approval and costs about $0.04 – propose only the few that matter) and place them with {{file:<id>}}. Our built-in design system rules (design-system) still apply. Source: github.com/vercel-labs/agent-skills (web-design-guidelines, Vercel) (MIT license).\n\nHow it works here:\n1. Fetch the latest rules with web_fetch: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vercel-labs/web-interface-guidelines/main/command.md\n2. Read the page to review (a file in the conversation, or ask the owner for it).\n3. Check it against all the rules. Report findings briefly, grouped by importance, each with the exact fix – in simple Hebrew.\n4. Offer to make the fixed version with make_file.\n\nOriginal skill text:\n# Web Interface Guidelines\n\nReview files for compliance with Web Interface Guidelines.\n\n## How It Works\n\n1. Fetch the latest guidelines from the source URL below\n2. Read the specified files (or prompt user for files/pattern)\n3. Check against all rules in the fetched guidelines\n4. Output findings in the terse `file:line` format\n\n## Guidelines Source\n\nFetch fresh guidelines before each review:\n\n```\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/vercel-labs/web-interface-guidelines/main/command.md\n```\n\nUse WebFetch to retrieve the latest rules. The fetched content contains all the rules and output format instructions.\n\n## Usage\n\nWhen a user provides a file or pattern argument:\n1. Fetch guidelines from the source URL above\n2. Read the specified files\n3. Apply all rules from the fetched guidelines\n4. Output findings using the format specified in the guidelines\n\nIf no files specified, ask the user which files to review."
